PatoLex -- DURABLE n_for() biennium/budget alias fix (PREVENT RECURRENCE only)
Task: teach merge_passes.n_for() the SAME production-dir <-> oracle-year alias _recall_allyears.py
uses, so a re-merge of a biennium/budget-named dir caps at the TRUE regular-session N instead of the
leading-year regex's tiny extra/budget-session N (the bug that corrupted 1901/1907/1909/1911).
CODE CHANGE ONLY. No data/merged/certified/DB writes. No git (parent commits).
================================================================================================

[2026-06-22 PT] PHASE: ROOT CAUSE | confirmed | OK
------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
merge_passes.n_for() used `re.search(r"production-(\d{4})", name)` -> grabs the FIRST 4-digit year.
For biennium dirs (production-1865-66, -1900-01, -1906-07, -1907-09, -1910-11) and budget sub-volume
dirs (production-1953-vol1-52chapters ...) the leading year is NOT the oracle regular session-year, so
ORACLE[leadingyear] was a tiny extra/budget row -> the merge capped N far too low (e.g. 1906-07 -> 1906
-> N=64 instead of 1907 -> 539). See LESSON_2026-06-22_biennial_volume_offset_merge_cap.md and the
merge-rerun-1901-1909-1911 / rebuild-1907-merge run logs.

[2026-06-22 PT] PHASE: FIX | centralize alias + alias-resolve n_for | OK
------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
NEW shared module: pipeline/year_dir_alias.py (single source of truth; sibling of config.py; imports
  NOTHING from ingest/ or analysis/ -> no import cycle). Exposes:
    YEAR_DIR_ALIAS    -- oracle-year -> dir basenames (VERBATIM copy moved out of _recall_allyears.py)
    BUDGET_OWNED_DIRS -- basenames the greedy odd-year glob must exclude
    DIR_TO_YEAR       -- inverse (basename -> oracle year), asserted 1:1 at import
    regular_oracle()  -- session_year -> total_chapters for REGULAR rows only (the cap rule)
    n_for_dir(name,oracle) -> (regular-N, year) for an aliased dir, else (None, None)
  Caps at the REGULAR-session N (read straight from the oracle TSV), NOT max-across-session-types --
  required because budget years (e.g. 1952 regular=14 vs extras 33/34) would otherwise over-admit.

merge_passes.py: loads year_dir_alias.py by absolute path via importlib (file is run as a standalone
  script; package import would be fragile). n_for() now consults the alias FIRST; aliased dirs resolve
  to their true regular-N+year; NORMAL production-<year>* dirs keep the ORIGINAL leading-year-regex
  behavior unchanged (ORACLE max-across-types). No interface change to n_for()'s (N, year) return.

_recall_allyears.py: its in-file BUDGET_OWNED_DIRS / YEAR_DIR_ALIAS definitions REPLACED by re-binds
  to the shared module (BUDGET_OWNED_DIRS = _alias.BUDGET_OWNED_DIRS, etc.) -- so merge and scoreboard
  CANNOT drift. Loaded by the same absolute-path importlib shim. No other logic touched.

